WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:02.320
You've probably heard of a pride of lions, a pod&nbsp;

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:06.800
of whales, a swarm of bees - maybe even
a murder&nbsp;of crows. But how about a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:11.080
parliament of owls? A shrewdness&nbsp;
of Apes? An obstinacy of Buffalo?

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:15.160
A bask of crocodiles or a convocation&nbsp;
of eagles? Wait why is this happening?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:17.960
Why should every species get&nbsp;
their own collective noun?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:22.240
No one could ever memorize all these.&nbsp;
The practice, called terms of venery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:26.080
dates back to the aristocratic hunting&nbsp;
traditions of the late Middle Ages.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:29.720
It was an&nbsp;insider's jargon to tell&nbsp;
who the real hunters were. Did you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:33.640
hear Roderick of Hertfordshire say pack of&nbsp;foxes?

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.240
Everyone knows it's called a skulk! Kind of like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:40.160
how modern sports fans have&nbsp;
their own slang, like...

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.040
Actually I don't know any sports slang. Over&nbsp;the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:45.040
years it developed into a playful&nbsp;
linguistic custom. A way to sound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:48.320
clever at social gatherings. Which&nbsp;
is basically what it still is today.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:52.560
It has no other real purpose. So the next&nbsp;
time you're at a party and someone says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:55.440
"actually it's a drift of pigs" - feel free to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.515
drift away. 

